avid","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"David::David::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"David"},"text":"These are the words of the Teacher, the son of David, king in Jerusalem:","verse":1}],"id":"David","label":"David","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"David::David::reviewed_unambiguous_name","scope":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","senseReview":"Reviewed complete original name headword and source subject. Article covers the biblical person(s), named place, or named spiritual figure denoted by this exact spelling; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"David","term":"David"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"David","sourceTitle":"David"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Jerusalem","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Jerusalem::Jerusalem::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Jerusalem"},"text":"These are the words of the Teacher, the son of David, king in Jerusalem:","verse":1},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Jerusalem","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Jerusalem::Jerusalem::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Jerusalem"},"text":"I, the Teacher, was king over Israel in Jerusalem.","verse":12},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Jerusalem","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Jerusalem::Jerusalem::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Jerusalem"},"text":"I said to myself, “Behold, I have grown and increased in wisdom beyond all those before me who were over Jerusalem, and my mind has observed a wealth of wisdom and knowledge.”","verse":16}],"id":"Jerusalem","label":"Jerusalem","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Jerusalem::Jerusalem::reviewed_unambiguous_name","scope":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","senseReview":"Reviewed complete original name headword and source subject. Article covers the biblical person(s), named place, or named spiritual figure denoted by this exact spelling; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Jerusalem","term":"Jerusalem"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Jerusalem","sourceTitle":"Jerusalem"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":["All Is “Vapor”"],"sourceClass":"theme-title","t":"h"},{"c":["A key word in Ecclesiastes is the Hebrew term ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," (“vapor,” often translated “meaningless”). This word expresses the core of the Teacher’s judgment concerning life in this world."],"sourceClass":"theme-body-fl","t":"p"},{"c":["At its root, ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," means physical “breath” or “vapor.” ",{"c":["Hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," is used seventy-eight times in the Old Testament, but in only three instances is the physical meaning clearly intended (",{"href":"?bref=Ps.62.9","r":"Ps.62.9","rawR":"Ps.62.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.62.9","value":"Ps.62.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 62:9"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Pr.21.6","r":"Pr.21.6","rawR":"Pr.21.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.21.6","value":"Pr.21.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Prov 21:6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.57.13","r":"Isa.57.13","rawR":"Isa.57.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.57.13","value":"Isa.57.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Isa 57:13"},"). In the other seventy-five instances, the word is used metaphorically to describe what is incomprehensible, futile, meaningless, false, transitory, or insubstantial. The term is often used to describe the insubstantiality, unreality, and worthlessness of false gods (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.32.21","r":"Deut.32.21","rawR":"Deut.32.21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.32.21","value":"Deut.32.21"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 32:21"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.17.15","r":"2Kgs.17.15","rawR":"2Kgs.17.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.17.15","value":"2Kgs.17.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 17:15"},"). In this sense, ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," is the opposite of “glory”—the substantial, weighty, and lasting presence of God. Sometimes the word ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," stands for the way life is fleeting and momentary, like vapor (",{"href":"?bref=Job.7.16","r":"Job.7.16","rawR":"Job.7.16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.7.16","value":"Job.7.16"}],"t":"ref","v":"Job 7:16"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.144.4","r":"Ps.144.4","rawR":"Ps.144.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.144.4","value":"Ps.144.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 144:4"},"). In other cases, it refers to the meaninglessness and frustration of life (",{"href":"?bref=Ps.78.33","r":"Ps.78.33","rawR":"Ps.78.33","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.78.33","value":"Ps.78.33"}],"t":"ref","v":"Pss 78:33"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.94.11","r":"Ps.94.11","rawR":"Ps.94.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.94.11","value":"Ps.94.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"94:11"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.49.4","r":"Isa.49.4","rawR":"Isa.49.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.49.4","value":"Isa.49.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Isa 49:4"},")."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["There is a long tradition of understanding ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," in Ecclesiastes as meaning “vanity,” not in the sense of thinking too highly of oneself, but in the sense of experiencing life as futile and without purpose or meaning. The ancient Greek translation of the Old Testament used the word ",{"c":["mataiotēs"],"sourceClass":"greek","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," which means “emptiness” or “futility.” The Latin Vulgate then translated ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," with the Latin word ",{"c":["vanitas"],"sourceClass":"latin","t":"span"},", which means “purposelessness, pointlessness, hollowness, worthlessness.” From the Vulgate comes the long-standing tradition of translating the word as “vanity” in English. Most translators today understand the word ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," in this sense: Life on earth has no apparent purpose or meaning, and all that we do is futile."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Some translators, however, understand ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," in Ecclesiastes to be referring primarily to the transitory impermanence of life. Vapor has no permanence or weight. This translation leads to a different understanding of Ecclesiastes: Life is not so much meaningless and futile as it is impermanent and fleeting. Many phrases in Ecclesiastes allude to the fleeting nature of human life and its efforts. In the New Testament, a similar thought can be found in ",{"href":"?bref=Jas.4.13-15","r":"Jas.4.13-15","rawR":"Jas.4.13-15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jas.4.13-15","value":"Jas.4.13-15"}],"t":"ref","v":"James 4:13-15"},". The understanding of ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," as “impermanence” in Ecclesiastes has merit in explaining the book coherently; it is, however, a minority view among scholars."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["The NLT translates ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," in Ecclesiastes as “meaningless.” However, while reading Ecclesiastes, it is helpful to keep in mind the root of the word (“breath” or “vapor”) that stands behind the metaphors."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["The brevity, frustration, and apparent meaninglessness of life can be depressing. This discouragement should motivate us toward working more wisely and diligently and enjoying life while we can. We have all observed the apparent absurdity of life’s tragedies, such as the success of a wicked fool or unchecked injustice. But these things come into proportion when we remember God’s sovereignty, because “God will judge us for everything we do, including every secret thing, whether good or bad” (",{"href":"?bref=Eccl.12.14","r":"Eccl.12.14","rawR":"Eccl.12.14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.12.14","value":"Eccl.12.14"}],"t":"ref","v":"Eccl 12:14"},")."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Passages for Further Study"],"sourceClass":"theme-refs-title","t":"h"},{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.32.21","r":"Deut.32.21","rawR":"Deut.32.21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.32.21","value":"Deut.32.21"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 32:21"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.17.15","r":"2Kgs.17.15","rawR":"2Kgs.17.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.17.15","value":"2Kgs.17.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 17:15"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Job.7.16","r":"Job.7.16","rawR":"Job.7.16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.7.16","value":"Job.7.16"}],"t":"ref","v":"Job 7:16"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.62.9","r":"Ps.62.9","rawR":"Ps.62.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.62.9","value":"Ps.62.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"Pss 62:9"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.78.33","r":"Ps.78.33","rawR":"Ps.78.33","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.78.33","value":"Ps.78.33"}],"t":"ref","v":"78:33"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.94.11","r":"Ps.94.11","rawR":"Ps.94.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.94.11","value":"Ps.94.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"94:11"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.144.4","r":"Ps.144.4","rawR":"Ps.144.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.144.4","value":"Ps.144.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"144:4"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Pr.21.6","r":"Pr.21.6","rawR":"Pr.21.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.21.6","value":"Pr.21.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Prov 21:6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Eccl.1.2","r":"Eccl.1.2","rawR":"Eccl.1.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.1.2","value":"Eccl.1.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"Eccl 1:2"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.30.7","r":"Isa.30.7","rawR":"Isa.30.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.30.7","value":"Isa.30.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Isa 30:7"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.49.4","r":"Isa.49.4","rawR":"Isa.49.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.49.4","value":"Isa.49.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"49:4"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.57.13","r":"Isa.57.13","rawR":"Isa.57.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.57.13","value":"Isa.57.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"57:13"}],"sourceClass":"theme-refs","t":"p"}],"id":"ThemeNote:AllIsVapor","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":9,"endVerse":12,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"AllIsVapor","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"ThemeNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"8e44b49b8362e87e4c68edc1d9dd9f34067c57242bf842b4cc95d531232e695c","sourceItemName":"AllIsVapor","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/ThemeNotes.xml","sourceOrder":135,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.1.2-9.12","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":"All Is “Vapor”","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"All Is “Vapor”","type":"ThemeNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Eccl.1.1","r":"Eccl.1.1","rawR":"Eccl.1.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.1.1","value":"Eccl.1.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:1"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Ecclesiastes never uses ",{"c":["the Teacher"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},"’s (Hebrew ",{"c":["Qoheleth"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"},") name, but ",{"c":["King David’s son"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," probably refers to Solomon (see also ",{"href":"?bref=Eccl.1.12","r":"Eccl.1.12","rawR":"Eccl.1.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.1.12","value":"Eccl.1.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:12"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Eccl.1.16","r":"Eccl.1.16","rawR":"Eccl.1.16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.1.16","value":"Eccl.1.16"}],"t":"ref","v":"16"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Eccl.2.4-9","r":"Eccl.2.4-9","rawR":"Eccl.2.4-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.2.4-9","value":"Eccl.2.4-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:4-9"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Eccl.1.1","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":1,"endVerse":1,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eccl.1.1","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[1],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"0380a11425075b56fcd4e681d97785b7cb5cd5de004c1b23a75276e3bc7b24cd","sourceItemName":"Eccl.1.1","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7962,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.1.1","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Eccl.1.1","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Eccl.1.2-11","r":"Eccl.1.2-11","rawR":"Eccl.1.2-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.1.2-11","value":"Eccl.1.2-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:2-11"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The Teacher begins by observing that each natural and historical activity is accentuated by its circular repetitive rhythm of coming and going, being and not being. This can lead to the despairing cry that ",{"c":["everything is meaningless"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},". The Teacher moves rapidly from subject to subject and finishes this section with the topic he started with, using a literary pattern that mirrors the circularity he describes in nature and in human history."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Eccl.1.2-11","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":1,"endVerse":11,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eccl.1.2-11","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[1],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"28dae97bd43ab0e575879b42357556172058afc824889fede7a72efa71382ede","sourceItemName":"Eccl.1.2-11","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7963,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.1.2-11","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Eccl.1.2-11","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Eccl.1.2","r":"Eccl.1.2","rawR":"Eccl.1.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.1.2","value":"Eccl.1.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:2"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Everything is meaningless . . . completely meaningless"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (literally ",{"c":["vapor of vapors, everything is vapor"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},"): Wherever “meaningless” occurs in this book, the Hebrew word ",{"c":["hebel"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," (“breath, vapor”) is behind it. The Teacher’s conclusion is that everything is a transitory and meaningless vapor."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Eccl.1.2","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":1,"endVerse":2,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eccl.1.2","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[1],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"e41241aab9f89498e83e8d27aa8a2c702faec62bf17b976b820d8a86ebdf471c","sourceItemName":"Eccl.1.2","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7964,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.1.2","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Eccl.1.2","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Eccl.1.3","r":"Eccl.1.3","rawR":"Eccl.1.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.1.3","value":"Eccl.1.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:3"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The Hebrew idiom ",{"c":["under the sun"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," is used twenty-nine times in Ecclesiastes. The NLT sometimes renders it with expressions such as “here on earth” (",{"href":"?bref=Eccl.2.18","r":"Eccl.2.18","rawR":"Eccl.2.18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.2.18","value":"Eccl.2.18"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:18"},") or “in this world” (",{"href":"?bref=Eccl.2.20","r":"Eccl.2.20","rawR":"Eccl.2.20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.2.20","value":"Eccl.2.20"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:20"},"). It suggests a view of life that is earthbound rather than reflecting God’s perspective."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Eccl.1.3","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":1,"endVerse":3,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":3}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eccl.1.3","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[1],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"84f201203f3825f03068e291ab4a62eee79491293e61d6d8e32430bc04f8e88a","sourceItemName":"Eccl.1.3","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7965,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.1.3","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Eccl.1.3","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Eccl.1.4","r":"Eccl.1.4","rawR":"Eccl.1.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.1.4","value":"Eccl.1.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Generations come"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," and ",{"c":["go"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},", and individual lives seem to pass away without significance."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Eccl.1.4","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":1,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":4}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eccl.1.4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[1],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"3fed77d0355a4e0a20780b8a83b4fdd06ce2e5fec663334584038992497a83c8","sourceItemName":"Eccl.1.4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7966,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.1.4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Eccl.1.4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Eccl.1.7-8","r":"Eccl.1.7-8","rawR":"Eccl.1.7-8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.1.7-8","value":"Eccl.1.7-8"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:7-8"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["the sea is never full . . . we are never satisfied:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," This image introduces the unfulfilling nature of life."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Eccl.1.7-8","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":1,"endVerse":8,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":7}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eccl.1.7-8","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[1],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"623a5515f4a15dfda9fda0bbfe28ab7feec77b66b9e64201c424e0eef65e954c","sourceItemName":"Eccl.1.7-8","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7967,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.1.7-8","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Eccl.1.7-8","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Eccl.1.11","r":"Eccl.1.11","rawR":"Eccl.1.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.1.11","value":"Eccl.1.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:11"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["no one will remember:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," We cannot count on anyone remembering anything that we think we have achieved."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Eccl.1.11","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":1,"endVerse":11,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":11}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eccl.1.11","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[1],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"51356ebbd7fed2cf9062fabe8681009b1930644411484bc97e208b87375bca77","sourceItemName":"Eccl.1.11","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7968,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.1.11","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Eccl.1.11","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Eccl.1.12-2.26","r":"Eccl.1.12-2.26","rawR":"Eccl.1.12-2.26","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.1.12-2.26","value":"Eccl.1.12-2.26"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:12–2:26"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The Teacher explores the relationships among wisdom, work, and pleasure."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Eccl.1.12-2.26","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":2,"endVerse":26,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":12}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eccl.1.12-2.26","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[1,2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"f1df7b09c103cb0bbaba207674689a6f7bd215c37972b51994f772c8d3ba9063","sourceItemName":"Eccl.1.12-2.26","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7969,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ECC","canonicalOSIS":"Eccl","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Eccl"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Eccl.1.12-2.26","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Eccl.1.12-2.26","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Eccl.1.13","r":"Eccl.1.13","rawR":"Eccl.1.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Eccl.1.13","value":"Eccl.1.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:13"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["God has dealt a tragic existence:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," The grim realities of human experience remind us of the Fall in Eden (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.3.1-24","r":"Gen.3.1-24","rawR":"Gen.3.1-24","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.3.1-24","value":"Gen.3.1-24"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gen 3"},"). Ecclesiastes does not dodge depressing descriptions of death, oppression, and loneliness; it meets them head-on and incorporates them into realistic conclusions."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Eccl.1.13","refs":[{"book":"ECC","endChapter":1,"endVerse":13,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":13}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Eccl.1.13","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ECC","sourceChapterCoverage":[1],"sourceI